W2 — one growth governor: the single chat.go anchor.

The blueprint says W2 needs no chat.go anchor, and structurally it does not:
every growth path already reaches growJob, the caps and the journal are live
with no wiring at all, and the ApplyRevision hole is closed whether or not this
patch lands. What this patch adds is the fourth check — the satisfaction gate —
for the paths that cannot be handed a client through their own signature
(ReplanOverrun's callers carry a plan function and a daily budget and nothing
else). The revision sentinel already passes its own client directly and is
gated without this.

Concurrent agents own cmd/aforge/chat.go this campaign, so it is left here
rather than applied. Rollback is deleting the line, or AFORGE_GROWTH_GATE=0.

--- a/cmd/aforge/chat.go
+++ b/cmd/aforge/chat.go
@@ -290,6 +290,13 @@
 	chatClient.WithUsageJournal(journalSpend)
 	taskClient.WithUsageJournal(journalSpend)
 	planClient.WithUsageJournal(journalSpend)
 	boostClients.WithUsageJournal(journalSpend)
+	// The positive stopping condition, installed once for every path that can
+	// grow a running job. It is asked last, after rounds, nodes and the daily
+	// rail have all passed, so on the common path it is never asked at all; the
+	// paths that ask it reach it through a package seam because they are
+	// reached through signatures that carry a plan function and a budget and
+	// have no client to give it. AFORGE_GROWTH_GATE=0 turns it off.
+	resident.SetGrowthSatisfier(resident.SatisfierFor(planClient))
 	// The standing watch is a host timer: installing it shells out to launchctl
 	// or systemctl and leaves something behind that outlives the process. A
 	// one-shot command may not do that to a machine, so headless never builds
